// `?.` on a built-in receiver: refused by name rather than compiled.
//
// It used to compile, and the compiled program segfaulted. The built-in
// method paths all dereference the receiver, and `safe` only ever stripped
// the `?` from the receiver's TYPE — so a null list reached a `->len` and
// the process died where the interpreters print `null`.
//
// A crash is the one answer a backend must never give. Until these paths
// are guarded, this is a refusal, which is what the rest of the backend
// does with everything it has not learned.
val xs: List<String>? = null
println(xs?.contains("a"))

val m: Map<String, Int>? = null
println(m?.containsKey("a"))

// The PROPERTY path was never broken: these three compile and answer null on
// all three engines. They are here to mark the boundary — the hole was in the
// method path alone, and a test that did not say so would leave the next
// reader guessing how wide it was.
println(xs?.size)
println(m?.size)
val s: String? = null
println(s?.length)
